Text carries `font-id` and `font-variant-id`, so a font choice can render.

`font_family` was stored and then ignored: `build_text_content` emitted
`font-family` and nothing else, and Penpot's renderer keys font loading off
`font-id`/`font-variant-id`. A text node carrying only a family renders in the
default, and it does so silently — the family string reads back verbatim through
`get_shape_details`, so the file looks correct and only the pixels disagree.
That made D3's type pairing undeliverable as written.

`text-font-attrs` in `app/common/types/text.cljc` is
`[:font-id :font-family :font-variant-id :font-size :font-weight :font-style]`,
read out of the running backend's own penpot.jar. The fields were always there;
nothing wrote two of them.

Catalogue ids are `gfont-` + the family lowercased with spaces hyphenated,
validated against 1719 of the 1958 entries in the shipped frontend bundle with
zero mismatches. `sourcesanspro` is the built-in default and is not a `gfont-`
id. `derive_font_id` applies that rule; `font_id` overrides it.

Values are strings, not keywords, so `content` stays on `:set` and patch 0002's
deliberate exclusion of `content` from the coercion tables is unaffected.

The catalogue lives in the frontend bundle and no backend or DB surface carries
it, so a well-formed id for a family Penpot does not have cannot be refused here
and renders the default with no error. The docstrings say so and name the check:
`export_frame_svg`, read `font-family` off the text element.

